{
  "case_id": "2009-08-992",
  "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The court of appeals refused to honor the resulting procedural default on the ground that it was inadequate because discretionary. This Court should clarify that the purpose of the adequate State grounds doctrine isn't to strip State courts of their equitable power to excuse procedural defaults, but simply to ensure that they don't discriminate against Federal claims under the guise of procedural ruling."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: I didn't understand the Third Circuit rule to equate discretion with inadequacy."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0000-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Where is that? I mean, why do we have to guess about it? Where -- where do you find that in its opinion?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I don't think that's a fair reading of this language, in and of itself, Your Honor. But it really doesn't matter for our position, and that's because the rule that was applied to this defendant was a discretionary rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 0.92,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your Honor, I think the problem here and the reason that we are at this point is that, whether the test is stated directly as being a per se ban on discretion or whether simply the standard is so high under an interpretation of \"firmly established\" that allows for virtually no deviation in results by the State courts, the effect is the same. It's to drive out the exercise of discretion by the State courts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I think, Your Honor, what we are presenting is that the nature of the State rule here was -- was such that the defendant had reasonable notice of what he was facing by escaping, whether or not it would have affected his subjective decision to escape, and therefore that the State ground can't be thrown out in Federal court on the ground of adequacy."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "25 States",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 0.91,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0000-0006",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If you look at the amicus brief filed on behalf of 25 States, you will see that that there are a great many cases where that's exactly the analysis that the courts have applied, and in many of those cases they have required the parties to place before them dozens and sometimes hundreds of other examples of the operation of a State procedural rule so that the lower Federal court can decide whether that --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0000-0011",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Well, do you want to modify then what you said?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So what you are disagreeing with is the Third Circuit's conclusion of what the status of Pennsylvania law was?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In fact, not a new question at all under State law, Your Honor. And we have cited several State court opinions --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May I ask one question before your time is gone? Has there ever been a precedent in Pennsylvania where they have applied the procedural default rule against a capital defendant who -- who was guilty of flight?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Commonwealth",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I first want to address what is really at stake here. The Commonwealth wants this Court to change the adequate State ground doctrine so that Mr. Kindler may be executed with no review by any court of his meritorious claims that his death sentence was unconstitutional."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: So is there anything we can say that will improve the situation? That's why I started out by saying maybe the best thing is to say nothing."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Lawry",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. LAWRY: Well, the -- the key is really consistent application and -- and looking to see whether the rules are being turned off and on. Like I would -- I would point the Court to Barr v. City of Columbia, which I think is a very good example, where there is maybe five people who make the same objection, and four of them get merits review, and the other person doesn't get merits review. Now, maybe there's some explanation somewhere for that, but this Court said: You know, this is not what we call adequacy; this is not consistent application."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Lawry",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0002-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Look at the relaxed waiver cases in the attachment to the brief. There are 51 cases over a 20-year period where the State courts reviewed every single issue on the merits in a capital case, regardless of what happened below."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Lawry",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0002-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In Federal court if it's held, as we argue, that the State court rulings were not adequate, that's true. And their argument is he should be executed with no review by any court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Eisenberg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. EISENBERG: Mr. Lawry's answer to the question about a mandatory forfeiture rule, Your Honors, I think is the crucial one here. When asked whether an automatic mandatory forfeiture rule would be adequate, he said yes. And I think that's the problem with just trying to remand this case without doing more, because that's where you wind up."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-992",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81617584150348851aa45f371ad3f8ba22f966e816b6e0981497e5966d429e5f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-816175841503-0002-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
